----- stdout -----
Emergency teardown: detach overlays immediately, best-effort cleanup

Usage: nails emergency [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -c, --config <PATH>
          Path to the configuration file (overrides binary-relative discovery)

      --countdown <SECS>
          Wait this many seconds before tearing down, printing a Ctrl+C abort hint.

          Omitted (the default) tears down immediately. The wait is never affected by `--quiet` or `--json`, so output flags never change the timing.

      --poweroff
          Power the machine off after the teardown completes.

          Powering off clears RAM, the only complete remedy for residue the teardown cannot remove. Mutually exclusive with `--reboot`.

  -v, --verbose...
          More diagnostics on stderr: -v for debug, -vv for trace

  -q, --quiet
          Print only errors and the final result

      --reboot
          Reboot into the decoy after the teardown completes.

          A reboot clears RAM. Mutually exclusive with `--poweroff`.

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour a stream only when it is a terminal, TERM is not `dumb` and NO_COLOR is unset or empty
          - always: Always colour, even when piped
          - never:  Never colour

          [default: auto]

      --lock-backend
          Close the encrypted backend after the teardown: lazy-unmount the hidden root and mark its device-mapper stack for deferred removal, wiping the master key from kernel memory when the last reference drops.

          Forces the close for this run regardless of the `[emergency] lock_backend` config key. Without it (and without the key) the hidden filesystem stays mounted and readable after the emergency, and the printed sequence closes it by hand.

      --ascii
          Use ASCII markers instead of Unicode symbols and box drawing

      --json
          Print machine-readable JSON on stdout

      --no-log-file
          Do not write the log file on the hidden volume (diagnostics still go to stderr)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

EXAMPLES:
  nails emergency                     Tear everything down immediately
  nails emergency --countdown 5       Wait 5s (Ctrl+C to abort), then tear down
  nails emergency --poweroff          Tear down, then power the machine off
  nails emergency --reboot            Tear down, then reboot into the decoy

